diff --git a/samples/subsys/pmci/pldm/i2c_loopback/README.rst b/samples/subsys/pmci/pldm/i2c_loopback/README.rst
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..783284ebf35
--- /dev/null
+++ b/samples/subsys/pmci/pldm/i2c_loopback/README.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+.. zephyr:code-sample:: pldm_i2c_loopback
+   :name: PLDM Type 0 over MCTP over SMBus/I2C (loopback)
+
+   Round-trip PLDM Type 0 across the DSP0237 MCTP-over-SMBus/I2C binding.
+
+Overview
+********
+
+This sample layers the full PLDM stack (``subsys/pmci/pldm``) on top of the
+standard DMTF **DSP0237** MCTP-over-SMBus/I2C transport binding
+(``CONFIG_MCTP_I2C``).
+
+Two ``struct mctp_binding_i2c`` instances are cross-wired through the binding's
+``link_write`` hook. Every packet one side transmits is emitted as a real
+SMBus block write frame::
+
+   [ 0x0F ] [ byte_count ] [ src_addr<<1|1 ] [ MCTP packet ] [ PEC ]
+
+and delivered verbatim to the peer's ``mctp_i2c_input()``, which performs the
+same command-code check, ``byte_count`` validation and SMBus PEC verification
+that the real I2C target-mode receive path uses. This exercises the entire
+DSP0237 wire format without needing an I2C controller that supports target
+mode (which QEMU does not yet provide).
+
+The requester then asks the responder for its TID, supported PLDM types and
+the base-type version, mirroring the in-memory ``type0_loopback`` sample but
+over a standards-compliant transport frame.
+
+Building and Running
+********************
+
+.. zephyr-app-commands::
+   :zephyr-app: samples/subsys/pmci/pldm/i2c_loopback
+   :board: qemu_riscv32
+   :goals: build run
+   :compact:
+
+Sample Output
+=============
+
+.. code-block:: console
+
+   *** Booting Zephyr OS build v4.3.0 ***
+   GetTID -> 0x01
+   GetPLDMTypes -> byte0=0x01
+   GetPLDMVersion(BASE) -> 1.1.0
+   PLDM over MCTP over I2C loopback OK
+
+Wire compatibility
+******************
+
+The framing produced by this binding matches the Linux kernel ``mctp-i2c``
+driver used by OpenBMC. Connecting two live QEMU instances still requires an
+I2C controller model with target-mode support (not yet in upstream QEMU); the
+UART / ``mctp-serial`` bridge remains the practical inter-QEMU path today.
